---
title: Penduline tit
---
# Penduline tit

The penduline tit is a small, masked songbird of reedbeds and riverside willows
across Europe and western Asia. The male suspends a thick-walled purse from a
drooping twig, felting seed down, willow catkin and spider silk into a fabric
strong enough to hold a hand's weight, and finishes it with a downward-facing
entrance tube. A false entrance beside the real one leads to a blind pocket,
which may delay a predator. Pairings are brief and either sex may desert, so the
elaborate structure is often finished and tended by one parent alone. See
[[remizidae]].
